  "page1": {
    "title": "Why We Don't Say Words the Way We Spell Them",
    "subtitle": "The hidden rules that make speech flow.",
    "body": "Say the words \"handbag\" or \"input\" quickly and listen closely. Many people actually say \"hambag\" and \"imput.\" The letters on the page did not change at all, but the <u>sounds</u> coming out of our mouths clearly did. This is not laziness or bad speech. It is a hidden rule of language quietly doing its job, smoothing the way our mouths move from one sound to the next.\n\nThese small shifts are called <mark>sound change</mark>, and they follow clear patterns. One common pattern is when a sound copies the sound next to it, so the two become easier to say together. The \"n\" in \"input\" leans toward the \"p\" and turns into an \"m,\" because the lips are already getting ready for \"p.\" Another pattern drops or blends sounds when keeping them all would be clumsy. In English, the \"k\" in \"knife\" was once spoken aloud, but over time our mouths quietly dropped it. Every language does this, and Korean does it in especially neat, rule-based ways. A Korean learner can map out the rules step by step and then predict exactly how a written form will sound.\n\nUnderstanding sound change is more useful than it first looks. It explains the wide gap between spelling and speech that confuses so many language learners. It shows that our writing often keeps an older, fuller form while <u>our mouths quietly take easier shortcuts</u>. This is why memorizing a spelling does not always tell you how to say a word. Most of all, it reveals that language is not careless when it shifts; it is following deep, shared rules we rarely notice. Once you can see those rules, the messy world of pronunciation suddenly looks less like chaos and more like a quiet, elegant <blank>.",
    "illustration": "../../assets/illustrations/2026-07/10.jpg",
    "illustration_caption": "Sounds shift to flow smoothly, even when the spelling stays the same.",
    "gloss_extra": [
      {
        "term": "pronunciation",
        "ko": "발음"
      },
      {
        "term": "clumsy",
        "ko": "어색한, 서투른"
      },
      {
        "term": "elegant",
        "ko": "우아한, 깔끔한"
      }
    ]
  },
